n)\ndescription: Fragmentation method used for MS/MS.\ninstructions: Use PSI-MS terms. If multiple methods, return a list.\n  Common methods:\n    HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422\n    CID → NT=CID;AC=MS:1002472\n    ETD → NT=ETD;AC=MS:1000598\n    EThcD → NT=EThcD;AC=MS:1002678\n    UVPD → NT=UVPD;AC=MS:1003247\n    stepped HCD → NT=HCD;AC=MS:1000422 (stepped HCD is still HCD)\n\nterm: collision energy\nsdrf_column: comment[collision energy]\nontology: MS:1000045\ndescription: Collision energy used for fragmentation.\npattern: {value} {unit} where unit is NCE, %, or eV.\n  Stepped HCD: {value} {unit};{value} {unit};{value} {unit}\n  Examples: "30 NCE", "30% NCE", "27 eV", "25 NCE;27 NCE;30 NCE"\ninstructions: Return null if not stated.\n\nterm: dia method\nsdrf_column: comment[dia method]\nontology: PRIDE:0000450\ndescription: Specific DIA method variant (only relevant for DIA datasets).\ninstructions: Return null for DDA XL-MS datasets (which is nearly all XL-MS).\n  If DIA is used, specify the variant (e.g. "SWATH", "MSe", "diaPASEF").\n\nterm: fragment mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[fragment mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000576\ndescription: Fragment ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "20 ppm", "0.02 Da")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical XL-MS values: 10–20 ppm for\n  Orbitrap instruments, 0.02–0.05 Da for ion trap.\n\nterm: precursor mass tolerance\nsdrf_column: comment[precursor mass tolerance]\nontology: PRIDE:0000575\ndescription: Precursor ion mass tolerance for the database search.\npattern: number + ppm or Da (e.g. "10 ppm", "5 ppm")\ninstructions: Return null if not stated. Typical: 5–10 ppm.\n\nterm: technical replicate\nsdrf_column: comment[technical replicate]\nontology: MS:1001808\ndescription: Identifier for the technical replicate (integer).\ninstructions: Describe the technical replication strategy found in the text\n  (e.g. "2 technical replicates per sample", "no technical replicates stated").\n  Actual integers are assigned per-file automatically.\n\nterm: data file\nsdrf_column: comment[data file]\nontology: MS:1003083\ndescription: Name of the raw data file (.raw, .wiff, .d).\ninstructions: This is always auto-derived from the PRIDE file list.\n  Describe the file naming convention you observe (e.g. "sample_condition_rep.raw").\n─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n\nReturn a single JSON object where each key is the exact sdrf_column string shown above.\nFor each key, return a dict with:\n  "value": the extracted value (string, list, or null)\n  "accession": ontology accession if resolvable, else null\n  "confidence": "high", "medium", or "low"\n  "evidence_quote": short verbatim quote from the text, or null\n  "evidence_location": location in the paper (e.g. "Methods"), or null'}, {'role': 'user', 'content': '[TITLE]\nCross-linking/mass spectrometry analysis of the AOC complex\n\n[ABSTRACT]\nAs a part of a study on how kinetochors are assembled at the centromeres of the chromosomes, cross-linking/mass spectrometry has been applied to investigate interactions between Okp1/ Ame1 heterodimer, which is part of the COMA complex,  and CENP-A from Saccharomyces cerevisiae.\n\n[METHODS]\nThe purified Ame1/Okp1/Cse4N complex at a concentration of 1.2 mg/ml was crosslinked with 150 μM BS3 (bis(sulfosuccinimidyl) suberate) in 50 mM HEPES, pH 7.5, 200 mM NaCl and 1 mM DTT and incubated for 2 h on ice. The reaction was stopped with 50 mM Tris-HCl, pH 7.5, and proteins were separated by SDS-PAGE electrophoresis. A gel band corresponding to the monomeric cross-linked Ame1/Okp1/Cse4N complex was excised from the gel and digested with trypsin (Pierce, Germany) (Shevchenko et al., 2006). The resulting tryptic peptides were extracted from the gel de-salted using C18 StageTips (Rappsilber et al., 2003).  LC-MS/MS analysis was conducted in triplicates, performed on an Orbitrap FusionTM LumosTM TribridTM mass spectrometer (Thermo Fisher Scientific, Germany) coupled on-line to Ultimate 3000 RSLCano Systems (Dionex, Thermo Fisher Scientific, Germany). The eluted peptides are ionized by an EASY-Spray source (Thermo Scientific). Mobile phase A consists of water, 0.1% v/v formic acid and mobile phase B consists of 80% v/v acetonitrile and 0.1% v/v formic acid. For CLMS analysis, peptides are loaded at a flow-rate of 0.3 μL/min and eluted at 0.2 μL/min using a linear gradient going from 2% mobile phase B to 40% mobile phase B over 139 min, followed by a linear increase from 45% to 95% mobile phase B in twelve min. The MS data is acquired in the data-dependent mode with the top-speed option. For each three-second acquisition cycle, the full scan mass spectrum was recorded in the Orbitrap with a resolution of 120,000. The ions with a precursor charge state between 3+ and 7+ were isolated and fragmented. Each cross-linked sample was analysed with two LC-MS/MS acquisitions, in one acquisition, peptides were fragmented using Higher-energy collisional dissociation (HCD) with 30% collision energy. The fragmentation spectra were then recorded in the Orbitrap with a resolution of 30000. Dynamic exclusion was enabled with single repeat count and 60-second exclusion duration.\n'}], 'model': 'gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17', 'store': True, 'temperature': 0.3}}
